# 12. Cycle Detection Template

Sometimes the problem only asks whether a cycle exists.

In that case, we do not necessarily need to store the entire ordering.

We can maintain a count of processed nodes.

```java
public boolean hasCycle(int vertices, int[][] edges) {

    List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();

    for (int i = 0; i < vertices; i++) {
        adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
    }

    int[] indegree = new int[vertices];

    for (int[] edge : edges) {
        int source = edge[0];
        int destination = edge[1];

        adj.get(source).add(destination);
        indegree[destination]++;
    }

    Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();

    for (int node = 0; node < vertices; node++) {
        if (indegree[node] == 0) {
            queue.offer(node);
        }
    }

    int processedNodes = 0;

    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {

        int current = queue.poll();

        processedNodes++;

        for (int neighbor : adj.get(current)) {

            indegree[neighbor]--;

            if (indegree[neighbor] == 0) {
                queue.offer(neighbor);
            }
        }
    }

    /*
     * If all nodes were processed, there is no cycle.
     *
     * If some nodes remain unprocessed, the graph
     * contains a cycle.
     */
    return processedNodes != vertices;
}
```

